# KNOWN GAP — RUST LANE. `gg check` reports no errors; the program then
# DOUBLE-FREES at runtime (rc 134, `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`).
# Twelve lines, using the language's own move operator.
#
#     int f(Box[Leaf] !b, int base):
#         Leaf fe = (*b).clone()      # <- owned Box param, DEAD at the clone
#         return base + fe.v
#     ...
#     print(f(^bx, 10))
#
# ⚠ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS MOVE-ELIGIBILITY AT THE CLONE, not the binder.
# An earlier filing of the self-host sibling named "a Box bound by `case
# Some(...)`" as the discriminator; that was measured WRONG. What actually
# separates the cells is whether the Box is MOVE-ELIGIBLE where `.clone()`
# runs -- the CoW table in AGENTS.md ("Ownership at Consuming Positions"):
#
#   borrowed, or owned-but-still-LIVE   -> self-host double-frees, Rust is fine
#   owned and DEAD at the clone (this)  -> RUST double-frees, self-host is fine
#
# So the two lanes fail on OPPOSITE cells of one axis, and the earlier
# "Rust gg is correct on the identical program" is false in general -- it was
# true only of the cells that had been sampled.
#
# ⚠ A `Box` exists precisely to make double-free impossible by construction:
# single owner, no implicit copy path, `^` or `.clone()` required at every
# assignment. This is that guarantee failing in the lowering of the operator
# that enforces it.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints 17.
# Found 2026-08-24 by the R44 Track-K confirming output-review; orchestrator-
# verified (`gg check` rc 0, run rc 134).

struct Leaf:
    int v

int box_move_param_clone(Box[Leaf] !b, int base):
    Leaf fe = (*b).clone()
    return base + fe.v

void main():
    Leaf f = Leaf(7)
    Box[Leaf] bx = Box(f)
    print(box_move_param_clone(^bx, 10))
